From: Matt Hargett <plaztiksyke@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 00:00:00 -0700
Subject: wasmedge no fatal signal handlers on watchos

watchOS terminates an app that installs a handler for SIGSEGV, SIGBUS
or SIGFPE ("sigaction on fatal signals is not supported"), and every
execution, instantiation included, constructs a Fault that installs
them: the first WasmEdge call trapped in libsystem_c. The Apple-mobile
interpreter checks bounds and division explicitly, so on watchOS the
handlers become no-ops.
---
 lib/system/fault.cpp |   18 ++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/lib/system/fault.cpp b/lib/system/fault.cpp
--- a/lib/system/fault.cpp
+++ b/lib/system/fault.cpp
@@ -19,6 +19,10 @@
 #include "system/winapi.h"
 #endif
 
+#if defined(__APPLE__)
+#include <TargetConditionals.h>
+#endif
+
 namespace WasmEdge {
 
 namespace {
@@ -29,13 +33,23 @@
 
 void disableHandler() noexcept;
 
-[[noreturn]] void rethrowSignal(int Signal) {
+[[noreturn, maybe_unused]] void rethrowSignal(int Signal) {
   disableHandler();
   std::raise(Signal);
   std::abort();
 }
 
-#if defined(SA_SIGINFO)
+#if defined(__APPLE__) && TARGET_OS_WATCH
+
+// watchOS terminates an app that installs a handler for a fatal signal
+// ("sigaction on fatal signals is not supported"). Nothing needs them
+// there: the Apple-mobile interpreter checks memory bounds and integer
+// division explicitly instead of relying on a trap.
+void enableHandler() noexcept {}
+
+void disableHandler() noexcept {}
+
+#elif defined(SA_SIGINFO)
 void signalHandler(int Signal, siginfo_t *Siginfo, void *) {
   if (!Fault::areSignalTrapsEnabled()) {
     rethrowSignal(Signal);
